A||||7||JC Webb||Opel Speedster, The Next Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||JC Webb||12-14-2000||08:47 PM||||Everyone needs to check Motor Trend's December issue and read about the prospects of the Opel Speedster being the next Fiero.  I was wondering what the forum thinks about this newest concept?  I think it would be a great car.  My only objection is that there would only be 3500 made each year.  Isn't it hard to make money on just 3500 units?||||reg||1
Z||000001||jerpro||12-15-2000||09:14 AM||||I come from barcelona, spain and I have been with peugeot and opel alll my life.<BR>It is a great car and I would not mind buying it if it made it to the us...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||12-15-2000||11:05 AM||||AutoWeek had a story on the Opal Tigra in December of 1998 as a potential Fiero replacement.  If GM will drop Oldsmobile for low sales, what will they do for the "new Fiero" at only 3500 units/year when the original was dropped in 88 because they didn't sell 50000 units.  With 3500 units to make a profit, I would expect GM to put a Corvette size price tag on the car, for profit motivation, which will kill any market for the car.  I bought my 87 Fiero in 87 because it was less than $16K.. 1/2 the price of the Corvette.  Would I buy a new Fiero for $30K?  No way!||||reg||3
Z||000003||Oreif||12-15-2000||11:43 AM||||Pontiac had to sell 30,000 per year to keep the Fiero plant open. In 1988 they sold around 24,000 - 26,000. Also last I heard Staurn was also trying to acquire the Opel Speedster too. If they make only 3500 per year they will most likely price it too high.<BR>They probably think they could compete with the S2000 and the MR2 Spyder, But I have heard sales are lower than projected on those cars. If it was priced right I'd actually think about getting one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Red2m4||12-15-2000||12:06 PM||||If the Fiero is resurected, I would like to to see it have a saturn badge.  I believe that saturn is more in tune with the technology and philosophy of the fiero than pontiac ever was.  (Low Price, good quality and value.)<P>I would buy a saturn before any other GM product, excluding vettes.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||12-15-2000||12:27 PM||||Saturn wants to rebody the car with plastic panels. The Speedster is loosley based on the Lotus elise , it uses a Vauxhall twin cam 4cyl. I don't think you will see it over here. I have read articles that pontiac has decided not to import the car , no real word on the saturn. No matter what they bring it in as , if it performs as well as the elise , or even close to ..Sign me up .<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||12-15-2000||02:07 PM||||Slammed, <BR>Check this link...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004628.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004628.html</A> <P>Some info from the Motor Trend article.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||12-15-2000||03:04 PM||||Even if the car were to be sold in the US as a Saturn (or Pontiac), neither would actually produce it, just add their own exteriors. It would still be produced in Jolly Olde (England, that is) in the Lotus plant and imported here. There is currently one assembly line set up, capable of 3500 units/year. To import it they would have to set up another 3500 unit assembly line. If they go to all this trouble, it will be a well thought out effort.||||reg||1
